# What the seeds of TestDifferential of the evaluator must reach: see stmttest.Reached.
# A line is the least number of the total of a kind over all seeds, a tab, and the kind.
# The numbers are written by hand, and a recount does not change them. They start from a third of
# the totals on 2026-11-26, when the cases of text came, and the cases of each seed went from 2,500
# to 3,600. A total below its number fails the test, and so does a kind that no seed reaches three
# times.
#
# A kind "x op y" is an operator whose operands were of the types x and y, with what it gave after
# the colon if it failed; "op with null" one with a NULL operand; "- of ..." a negation; "case: ..."
# the outcome of a whole case, and "case: refused, ..." its refusal, without the number of an
# argument. A sum or a difference of an integer and a real cannot be infinite, nor a real divided by
# an integer, so those kinds are not here.
#
# The edges of arithmetic: "x op y: exactly the largest integer" or "... smallest integer" is an
# integer result that is that bound; "x op y: subnormal, not zero" a real result below the
# smallest normal real that is not zero; "the smallest integer / a negative integer other than -1"
# a quotient of the smallest integer that fits; and "an error on the right of null" an operator
# whose left operand is NULL and whose right one fails, where SQLite gives NULL. A quotient that is
# exactly the largest integer and a real divided by an integer that is subnormal are too rare in
# the seeds to require.
#
# The kinds of text: "|| ..." a concatenation of two texts, of which one or both are empty, or with
# NULL; "lower of text, ASCII x, changed y" a call of lower whose text has only ASCII or not, and
# that changes a letter or not, and "lower of a letter that is not ASCII and has a lower case in
# Unicode" one that SQLite leaves as it is; "hex of ..." a call of hex of what it names. "f: at the
# limit" is an operation whose allocation in SQLite is exactly the limit, which SQLite makes, and
# "f: text longer than the length limit" one whose allocation is longer, which SQLite refuses with
# `string or blob too big`. "case: deeper than 900" is a case of text whose tree is deeper than 900
# levels. A refusal of an argument of lower, other than for a column, is too rare in the seeds to
# require.
